Good question, I had to check that :) Turns out that docker is clever enough to store random ranges in such cases. Here are inspection results from some tests (also for me for later):

`docker run -p 80 ...`:
```
    "PortBindings": {
        "80/tcp": [
            {
                "HostIp": "",
                "HostPort": ""
            }
        ]
    },
```

`docker run -p 80-85:80 ...`:
```
    "PortBindings": {
        "80/tcp": [
            {
                "HostIp": "",
                "HostPort": "80-85"
            }
        ]
    },
```

`docker run -p 90-95:80-85 ...`:
```
    "PortBindings": {
        "80/tcp": [
            {
                "HostIp": "",
                "HostPort": "90"
            }
        ],
        "81/tcp": [
            {
                "HostIp": "",
                "HostPort": "91"
            }
        ],
        ...
        "85/tcp": [
            {
                "HostIp": "",
                "HostPort": "95"
            }
        ]
    },
```

So you're right, this is pretty simple to implement, also w.r.t. idempotency.

I'm now a bit hesistant to backport this, though, since this change would cause containers using something like `80-85:85` right now to be recreated. Maybe it's better to fix this in `devel` and let this only show up in Ansible 2.10 and later.
